`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`
Type aliases (`type mrKey = domain.MRKey`) versus defined types (`type mrKey domain.MRKey`) matter here: an alias makes `mrKey` and `domain.MRKey` completely interchangeable — same underlying type, same method set, usable as map keys against either name. A defined type would need explicit conversions everywhere. This is exactly the tool for "promote a private type to a canonical exported one without touching every call site."

The GraphQL vs REST split for `UpdatedAt` (string needing `time.Parse` vs `*time.Time` needing a nil-check) mirrors why `CreatedAt` already had two different handling blocks in the same mapper file — GitLab's REST and GraphQL APIs serialize timestamps differently, and the codebase already had the pattern to copy rather than invent.
`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`

## Summary

Implemented the pure type/port foundation for the incremental-fetch epic (ADR-0005):
- `domain.MRKey{ProjectID, IID int}` — promoted from `gitlabadpt`'s private `mrKey` via a type alias
